! Stress exponence — twolc rules for the notation layer.
!
! Gravis, gemination and macron are stored directly in the lexc as the lemma's
! citation (long / barytone) grade. The one productive alternation modelled here
! is the participial ACCENT SHIFT (Rinkevičius 2009, docs/AKZENT.md): a mobile
! stem de-accents under a strong ending.
!
! The strong participle endings carry a DEAC trigger on the surface tape, placed
! at the stem|ending boundary (…stem DEAC ending…). A de-accenting stem shortens
! its accented (last) stem syllable's long/gravis vowel before the trigger; the
! trigger then deletes (Alphabet: DEAC:0). Barytone stems route through endings
! without the trigger and keep their accent.

Alphabet
 a à ā b c d ḑ e è ē f g ģ h i ì ī j k ķ l m n ņ o ò ō p r ŗ s š t ţ u ù ū w z ž
 DEAC:0 ;

Sets
 Stem = a à ā b c d ḑ e è ē f g ģ h i ì ī j k ķ l m n ņ o ò ō p r ŗ s š t ţ u ù ū w z ž ;

Rules

! A de-accenting stem is entirely unstressed, so every long/gravis vowel up to
! the trigger shortens (not just the last syllable): ainawīdinant+DEAC+ī →
! ainawidinantī.
"Shorten long/gravis stem vowels before the deaccent trigger"
 Vx:Vy <=> _ Stem* DEAC: ;
   where Vx in (ā ē ī ō ū à è ì ò ù)
         Vy in (a e i o u a e i o u)
   matched ;
